# SPA fallback, scoped to the two routes that are NOT prerendered.
#
# Every other client route is emitted as a real HTML file by
# scripts/prerender.js (/, /about, /contact, /sitemap, /terms, /privacy) and is
# served as a static asset. A blanket `/* /index.html 200` is deliberately not
# used: it shadows those files on static servers that apply the fallback before
# checking for an asset, and it also answers every unknown URL with a 200 app
# shell — soft 404s, which Google treats as a quality signal.
#
# Omitting the catch-all is necessary but NOT sufficient: "If your project does
# not include a top-level 404.html file, Pages assumes that you are deploying a
# single-page application" and serves / for every unmatched path, with a 200.
# public/404.html is what actually makes an unknown URL return a 404 — do not
# delete it thinking it is decorative.
#
# Adding a client route means either adding it to ROUTES in
# scripts/prerender.js, or adding a fallback line here.
#
# These point at the app shell, not index.html: index.html is the prerendered
# landing page, and serving it here would hand every bike detail page a canonical
# pointing at the homepage before JS runs. app-shell.html is the bare shell.
#
# The target is `/app-shell`, WITHOUT the .html. Pages canonicalises HTML assets
# to their extensionless path, and it applies that to a rewrite destination too:
# with `/app-shell.html` as the target these rules stopped being 200 rewrites and
# became 308 redirects to /app-shell, which drops the :id and lands every bike
# detail URL on a route that does not exist. Verify with
# `npx wrangler pages dev dist` after touching this file — `curl -sS -o /dev/null
# -w "%{http_code}" /bikes/anything` must print 200, not 308.
#
# /unsubscribe sits above the splat rule deliberately: Pages evaluates in order
# and warns about static rules placed below wildcards.
/unsubscribe  /app-shell   200
/bikes/*      /app-shell   200
